<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-09">

<title>Week 9: Partial Differential Equations and Their Classification</title>

<ol>
  <caption>Learning Outcomes</caption>
    <li><p>Define a partial differential equation (PDE).</p></li>
    <li><p>Distinguish between ODEs and PDEs.</p></li>
    <li><p>Determine the order and linearity of a PDE.</p></li>
    <li><p>Classify second-order PDEs as elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic.</p></li>
    <li><p>Interpret PDEs in physical applications.</p></li>
    <li><p>Understand the role of boundary and initial conditions.</p></li>
    <li><p>Analyze simple PDE models arising in science and engineering.</p></li>
</ol>

<subsection xml:id="w9-intro">
<title>Introduction to Partial Differential Equations</title>

<definition>
<title>Partial Differential Equation</title>
<statement>
<p>A partial differential equation (PDE) is an equation involving an unknown function of several independent variables and one or more partial derivatives.</p>
<md>F(x,y,u,u_x,u_y,u_{xx},u_{xy},\ldots)=0</md>
</statement>
</definition>

<example>
<title>ODE versus PDE</title>
<statement>
<md>\frac{dy}{dt}=y</md>
<p>This is an ODE because there is only one independent variable.</p>
<md>u_t=u_{xx}</md>
<p>This is a PDE because the solution depends on both position and time.</p>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-order">
<title>Order and Linearity</title>

<definition>
<title>Order</title>
<statement>
<p>The order of a PDE is determined by its highest derivative.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<table>
<title>Examples</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Equation</cell><cell>Order</cell><cell>Linear?</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_t=u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>Yes</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_t=uu_x</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>No</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>Yes</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-classification">
<title>Classification of Second-Order PDEs</title>

<md>
Au_{xx}+Bu_{xy}+Cu_{yy}+Du_x+Eu_y+Fu=G
</md>

<md>
\Delta=B^2-4AC
</md>

<image width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=1cm]
\node[draw,rounded corners] (pde)
{Second-Order PDE};

\node[draw,below left=1.5cm and 2cm of pde]
(ell)
{Elliptic};

\node[draw,below=1.5cm of pde]
(par)
{Parabolic};

\node[draw,below right=1.5cm and 2cm of pde]
(hyp)
{Hyperbolic};

\draw[-&gt;] (pde)--(ell);
\draw[-&gt;] (pde)--(par);
\draw[-&gt;] (pde)--(hyp);

\node[left=.2cm of ell]
{$\Delta&lt;0$};

\node[right=.2cm of par]
{$\Delta=0$};

\node[right=.2cm of hyp]
{$\Delta&gt;0$};
\end{tikzpicture}

    </latex-image>
</image>

<table>
<title>PDE Classification</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Condition</cell><cell>Type</cell><cell>Example</cell><cell>Interpretation</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\Delta&lt;0</m></cell><cell>Elliptic</cell><cell><m>u_{xx}+u_{yy}=0</m></cell><cell>Equilibrium</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\Delta=0</m></cell><cell>Parabolic</cell><cell><m>u_t=ku_{xx}</m></cell><cell>Diffusion</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\Delta&gt;0</m></cell><cell>Hyperbolic</cell><cell><m>u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>Wave propagation</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-physical">
<title>Physical Interpretation</title>

<table>
<title>Common PDE Models</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Equation</cell><cell>Application</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{xx}+u_{yy}=0</m></cell><cell>Electrostatics and steady-state heat flow</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_t=ku_{xx}</m></cell><cell>Heat conduction and diffusion</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>String vibration and sound propagation</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-bc-ic">
<title>Boundary and Initial Conditions</title>

<table>
<title>Boundary Conditions</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Type</cell><cell>Form</cell><cell>Meaning</cell></row>
<row><cell>Dirichlet</cell><cell><m>u(a,t)=A</m></cell><cell>Value specified</cell></row>
<row><cell>Neumann</cell><cell><m>u_x(a,t)=A</m></cell><cell>Flux specified</cell></row>
<row><cell>Robin</cell><cell><m>au+bu_x=c</m></cell><cell>Mixed condition</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>

<md>u(x,0)=f(x)</md>
<p>The initial condition specifies the state of the system at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-separation">
<title>Separation of Variables</title>

<definition>
<title>Separated Solution</title>
<statement>
<md>u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)</md>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Substituting a product solution into a PDE often reduces the problem to two ordinary differential equations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-heat-example">
<title>Heat Equation Example</title>

<example>
<title>Heat Equation on a Finite Rod</title>
<statement>
<p>Solve</p>
<md>
u_t=u_{xx}, \qquad 0&lt;x&lt;L,
</md>
<p>with boundary conditions</p>
<md>
u(0,t)=0,
\qquad
u(L,t)=0,
</md>
<p>and initial condition</p>
<md>
u(x,0)=f(x).
</md>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>Assume a separated solution</p>
<md>
u(x,t)=X(x)T(t).
</md>

<p>Substituting gives</p>
<md>
XT'=X''T.
</md>

<p>Dividing by <m>XT</m> yields</p>
<md>
\frac{T'}{T}=\frac{X''}{X}=-\lambda.
</md>

<p>Two ordinary differential equations follow:</p>
<md>
X''+\lambda X=0,
</md>
<md>
T'+\lambda T=0.
</md>

<p>The boundary conditions imply</p>
<md>
X_n(x)=\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right).
</md>

<p>The time-dependent factors are</p>
<md>
T_n(t)=e^{-(n\pi/L)^2t}.
</md>

<p>Therefore</p>
<md>
u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n
\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
e^{-(n\pi/L)^2t}.
</md>
</solution>
</example>

<image width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[line width=3pt] (0,0)--(6,0);

\fill (0,0) circle (3pt);
\fill (6,0) circle (3pt);

\node[above] at (0,0) {$u(0,t)=0$};
\node[above] at (6,0) {$u(L,t)=0$};

\node[below] at (3,0) {Rod};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
<remark>
<title>Interpretation</title>
<p>Each Fourier mode decays exponentially. Higher-frequency modes decay more rapidly, causing the temperature profile to become smoother over time.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w9-matlab">
<title>MATLAB Visualization of Heat Diffusion</title>

<program language="matlab">
L = 1;
x = linspace(0,L,100);
t = linspace(0,0.5,100);
[X,T] = meshgrid(x,t);
U = exp(-pi^2*T).*sin(pi*X);
surf(X,T,U)
xlabel('Position')
ylabel('Time')
zlabel('Temperature')
title('Heat Equation Solution')
shading interp
colorbar
</program>
</subsection>

<summary>
<p>Partial differential equations model quantities that vary in both space and time. Second-order PDEs are classified as elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic. The heat equation provides a fundamental example of a parabolic PDE and can be solved using separation of variables and Fourier series.</p>
</summary>

</section>
